-rw-r--r-- 1 experimenter experimenter 9993 Jul 25 01:23 AGENTS.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 agents\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 extensions\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 skills\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784949933764},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4e91897441e54eee94e4c375","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/skills/ /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/agents/"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":212,"output":125,"cacheRead":32896,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":33233,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784949933764,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-2cc05fd8-6f4c-4ee9-81e2-71e549fd6bf7","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4e91897441e54eee94e4c375","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/agents/:\ntotal 16\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 experimenter experimenter 7841 Jul 25 01:23 refactor.md\n\n/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/skills/:\ntotal 20\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 green\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 red\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 01:23 test-list\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784949937690},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c53f067cbdf84637aded1e85","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":384,"output":78,"cacheRead":33152,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":33614,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784949937692,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-cab52cab-e5b0-4a43-a8c1-2e92fce42205","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_c53f067cbdf84637aded1e85","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: test-list\ndescription: TDD Test List Phase - Create a comprehensive test list covering every example and rule from the specification\n---\n\n# TDD Test List Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Test List Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to create a comprehensive test list.\n\n## Your Mission\n\nCreate a test list using `it.todo()` that covers **every rule and every example** from the specification:\n1. Read the specification (`prompt.md`) thoroughly -- every rule, every example, every clarifying question (?)\n2. Turn each example into at least one `it.todo()` test case\n3. Order tests from simplest to most complex\n4. Use `it.todo()` only -- NO executable tests yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Feature\nRead the complete specification. Pay special attention to integration examples and clarifying\nquestions (marked with ?) -- these disambiguate rules that may seem open to interpretation in isolation.\n- What are all the operations the system must support?\n- What rules govern each operation?\n- Which examples in the spec illustrate these rules?\n\n### Step 2: Identify Test Cases from the Spec\nWalk through the specification section by section. For each rule and each example:\n- Create a test case that verifies the described behavior\n- Include the **expected values from the spec** in the test description\n- If a clarifying question (?) resolves an ambiguity, create a test for the clarified interpretation\n- If the spec uses an example-mapping format (rules, examples, questions), every listed example must have a corresponding test\n\n### Step 3: Order Tests (Simple -> Complex)\nArrange tests in increasing complexity:\n1. Simplest case (often empty/zero/single item)\n2. Individual rules in isolation\n3. Rules with modifiers\n4. Combinations of multiple rules\n5. Multi-step scenarios (e.g., operations that reference earlier results)\n\n### Step 4: Write Test File\nCreate the test file with `it.todo()` entries:\n\n```typescript\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { functionName } from \"./implementation.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Feature Name\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"should [behavior] -- [expected value from spec]\");\n  it.todo(\"should [next behavior] -- [expected value from spec]\");\n  // ... ordered simple -> complex, covering ALL spec examples\n});\n```\n\n### Step 5: Provide Summary\n\nAfter creating the test list, output:\n\n```\nTest List Created:\n**Feature**: [feature name]\n**Test File**: [filename].spec.ts\n**Tests**: [count]\n\n**Test Cases** (ordered simple -> complex):\n1. [first test description]\n2. [second test description]\n3. [third test description]\n...\n\n**Next Step**: Invoke `red` skill to activate the first test.\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Cover **every spec example** with at least one test\n- Cover **every operation** described in the spec\n- Give **every clarifying question (?)** a corresponding test\n- Order tests **simple -> complex**\n- Use `it.todo()` for all tests\n- Include **expected values** in descriptions\n- Keep tests **independent**\n- One behavior per test\n\n### DON'T\n- Write executable tests (use `it.todo()`)\n- Think about implementation instead of behavior\n- Miss an entire operation described in the spec\n- Order randomly\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing the test list, proceed to Red phase:\n\n```\nTest List Phase Complete. Proceeding to Red phase with the first test.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784949940267},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_11307b6246514a989cb0bc60","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/skills/red/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":835,"output":77,"cacheRead":33536,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":34448,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784949940268,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c59c9cd9-ad19-44e9-a7a6-17f688d12303","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_11307b6246514a989cb0bc60","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: red\ndescription: TDD Red Phase - Activate ONE test from the test list and make it fail with explicit predictions\n---\n\n# TDD Red Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Red Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to activate ONE test and make it fail.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Activate exactly ONE test from the test list\n2. Make explicit predictions about how it will fail\n3. Verify the test fails for the right reason\n4. Maintain strict discipline - NO implementation during Red phase\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Red Phase Rules\n\n- **One test at a time**: Convert exactly ONE `it.todo()` to executable test\n- **All other tests remain as `it.todo()`**: Never have more than one failing test\n- **Two-stage failure**: First compilation error, then runtime/assertion error\n- **Make predictions**: Explicitly state expected failures before running tests\n- **No implementation**: Don't write code to make test pass yet\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Activate One Test\n\nIdentify the next `it.todo()` and convert it to executable test code:\n\n```typescript\n// Convert from:\nit.todo(\"should return 0 for empty input\");\n\n// To:\nit(\"should return 0 for empty input\", () => {\n  expect(calculate(\"\")).toBe(0);\n});\n```\n\nLeave all other tests as `it.todo()`.\n\n### Step 2: Predict Compilation Error\n\nBefore running the test, state your prediction:\n\n```\nRed Phase - Compilation Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n- Expected: Compilation error\n- Reason: Function `calculate` doesn't exist yet\n- Error: \"Cannot find name 'calculate'\"\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Test - Verify Compilation Error\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Compilation error as predicted, OR\n- Prediction wrong -> STOP and explain discrepancy\n\n### Step 4: Create Empty Function\n\nCreate minimal function stub (no logic):\n\n```typescript\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return undefined as unknown as number; // Intentionally wrong\n};\n```\n\n### Step 5: Predict Runtime Error\n\nBefore running again, state your prediction:\n\n```\nRed Phase - Runtime Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n- Expected: Runtime assertion error\n- Expected value: 0\n- Actual value: undefined\n- Diff:\n  Expected: 0\n  Received: undefined\n```\n\n### Step 6: Run Test - Verify Runtime Error\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Assertion error as predicted, OR\n- Prediction wrong -> STOP and explain discrepancy\n\n### Step 7: Report Completion\n\nYou MUST output the full Step 7 block verbatim with `Correct` or `Incorrect`\nchosen for each prediction. Do not abbreviate. Do not collapse the two\nprediction lines into one.\n\n**Why this format matters:** The block is mechanically parsed to compute\n`predictions_correct_rate`. The parser expects two lines matching\n`(- |Correct|Incorrect)` per cycle -- one for the compilation prediction,\none for the runtime prediction. Collapsing them into a single line, summarizing\nthem as \"both correct\", or skipping the block entirely drops the predictions\ncount for this cycle to zero. Format consistency here directly drives a metric\nthe experiment measures.\n\n```\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Test Activated**: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n**Compilation Prediction**: Cannot find name 'calculate' Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected 0, received undefined Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase.\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Activate exactly ONE test at a time\n- Make explicit predictions before running tests\n- Verify test fails for the right reason\n- Keep all other tests as `it.todo()`\n\n### DON'T\n- Activate multiple tests\n- Skip making predictions\n- Write implementation to make test pass\n- Continue if prediction fails without explanation\n\n## Prediction Failure Protocol\n\nIf your prediction was wrong:\n\n```\nPrediction Failed:\n- Predicted: [what you expected]\n- Actual: [what happened]\n- Discrepancy: [explanation]\n\nInvestigating the discrepancy before proceeding.\n```\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Red phase, proceed to Green phase:\n\n```\nRed Phase Complete. Proceeding to Green phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784949943348},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_33ebe744eaf849e392ee0287","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/.pi/skills/green/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":934,"output":78,"cacheRead":34432,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":35444,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784949943348,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-15109654-3e91-4b84-add9-1704d529a1f0","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_33ebe744eaf849e392ee0287","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: green\ndescription: TDD Green Phase - Implement minimal code to make the failing test pass\n---\n\n# TDD Green Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Green Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to make the failing test pass with MINIMAL code.\n\n## Why minimality matters\n\nThe Green Phase deliberately writes the smallest implementation that turns the active test green -- even hardcoded returns, even obviously-incomplete logic. This is not laziness or naivete:\n\n- It **exposes refactoring opportunities** in the next phase. When the implementation does exactly one thing, structural smells are visible. Over-implemented green code looks \"already clean\" and hides design problems.\n- It **prevents premature generalization**. The shape of the right abstraction emerges from the second and third test, not the first. Implementing for hypothetical future tests locks in a design before its constraints are known.\n- It **keeps the red-green-refactor cycle short**. Each cycle pays a cost in tokens and context; over-implementing in green collapses several future cycles into one large one and loses the per-test feedback loop.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Implement the **minimal code** necessary to make the failing test pass\n2. Use the **simplest possible solution** (hardcoded values are acceptable)\n3. Avoid adding features for future tests\n4. Verify all tests pass\n5. NO optimization, NO refactoring yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Green Phase Rules\n\n- **Minimal code only**: Just enough to pass the current test\n- **Baby steps**: Make the smallest possible change\n- **No future features**: Don't implement what future tests might need\n- **Simple is better**: Hardcoded returns are perfectly fine\n- **Tests must pass**: Verify all tests are green\n- **No refactoring yet**: Save improvements for Refactor phase\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Failing Test\n\nUnderstand what the test expects:\n- What input does the test provide?\n- What output does it expect?\n- What is the **simplest** way to produce that output?\n\n### Step 2: Write Minimal Implementation\n\nImplement **only what's needed** to make the current test pass:\n\n```typescript\n// For first test \"should return 0 for empty input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return 0; // Minimal - just make the test pass\n};\n\n// For second test \"should return number for single input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  return parseInt(input); // Still simple\n};\n\n// For third test \"should add two numbers\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  const numbers = input.split(\",\");\n  if (numbers.length === 1) return parseInt(numbers[0]);\n  return parseInt(numbers[0]) + parseInt(numbers[1]); // Only now add logic\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Tests\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Current test now passes\n- All previous tests still pass\n\n### Step 4: Verify No Over-Implementation\n\nCheck yourself:\n- Did I implement features for future tests? -> Remove them\n- Did I add logic not demanded by current test? -> Remove it\n- Did I optimize prematurely? -> Simplify\n- Did I refactor existing code? -> Revert, save for Refactor phase\n\n### Step 5: Report Completion\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete:\n**Implementation**: [brief description of what was added]\n**Result**: All tests now pass ([X] passing)\n**Approach**: [explain why this is minimal]\n\nProceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n\n## Minimal Implementation Strategies\n\n### Hardcoded Returns (Preferred for Early Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\nreturn 0; // Perfect - minimal\n```\n\n### Simple Conditionals (When Multiple Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return number for single input\"\nif (input === \"\") return 0;\nreturn parseInt(input); // Still simple\n```\n\n### Generalization (Only When Forced)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should add multiple numbers\" - NOW generalize\nreturn input.split(\",\").reduce((sum, n) => sum + parseInt(n), 0);\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Write minimal code to make test pass\n- Use hardcoded values when appropriate\n- Take baby steps\n- Verify all tests pass\n\n### DON'T\n- Implement beyond what tests demand\n- Add features for future tests\n- Optimize prematurely\n- Refactor during Green phase\n\n## Psychological Resistance\n\nYou will feel resistance:\n- **\"This is too simple\"** -> That's correct! Minimal is the way\n- **\"Hardcoded values feel wrong\"** -> They're exactly right for early tests\n- **\"I should implement ahead\"** -> Resist this strongly\n- **\"This is inefficient\"** -> Actually accelerates development\n\nTrust the process. Simple steps compound into elegant solutions.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Green phase, proceed to Refactor phase:\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete. Proceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784949946015},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the situation. The implementation already exists and all 48 tests are passing. The task is to:\n\n1. Follow the TDD workflow (test-list, red, green, refactor for each test)\n2. Produce all the mandatory markers\n3. End with experiment-done.txt containing \"DONE\"\n\nSince the tests are already passing and the implementation is complete, my role here is to follow the workflow that produces the proper markers for the measurement pipeline to track. I'll need to:\n\n1. Start with the Test List phase (mark `## Test List`)\n2. Go through Red/Green/Refactor cycles for each test\n3. End with experiment-done.txt\n\nLooking at the test file, all 48 tests are already implemented (not as `it.todo()` but as executable `it()`). So all 48 are already \"activated\" and passing.\n\nThe workflow says:\n- If a test already passes, write `## Red -- Test N: <test description> ... Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.`\n\nBut the workflow is set up assuming tests start as `it.todo()`. The current state is all tests are written and passing.\n\nLet me approach this pragmatically: Since this is an experiment measuring TDD discipline, I need to simulate the full TDD cycle by writing the markers for each test, but I cannot actually \"un-implement\" the code (that would defeat the experiment). The right interpretation is to walk through each test as if it had been done in TDD, producing all the mandatory markers and subagent calls.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully:\n- The skill says \"If a test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\"\n- The measurement pipeline tracks cycles based on `## Red` markers\n- The instructions say \"Continue autonomously through ALL tests in the test list until you have written experiment-done.txt\"\n- The mandatory format requires `## Red`, `## Green`, and subagent calls\n\nThe most pragmatic interpretation: For each test, I'll produce the Red marker, the Green marker, and call the refactor subagent. Since the tests all pass already, my predictions should be that they would compile and pass.\n\nActually let me re-read carefully. The instructions for the red phase say:\n- \"Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\"\n\nSo if a test already passes (which all of them do), I should:\n1. Output the Red marker with \"Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\"\n\nBut that means I'd skip green and refactor entirely, which seems to violate the measurement pipeline (it counts Green and refactor markers).\n\nLet me think... The instruction says \"Do not skip them when a test already passes -- in that case, write:\"\n\nOK so when a test already passes, we DO need to emit the prediction lines. But the green/refactor are skipped.\n\nLooking again:\n> Do not abbreviate, summarize, or collapse them. Do not skip them when a test already passes --\n> in that case, write:\n> \n> ```\n> ## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n> \n> Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\n> ```\n\nWait, that doesn't include the prediction lines. Let me look at this more carefully...\n\nActually looking at the format in AGENTS.md:\n```\n## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n\nTest already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\n```\n\nAnd the format with predictions:\n```\n## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n\n<prediction and failure verification steps>\n\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Compilation Prediction**: <what you expect> Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: <what you expect> Correct\n\n<Result summary>\n```\n\nThe two prediction lines are \"mandatory for every Red cycle that fails\". For cycles where the test already passes, we just emit the simplified block. But that means `predictions_correct` and `predictions_total` would both be 0 because there are no prediction lines.\n\nOK, but the task is to complete the exercise, and the markers are needed for the measurement pipeline. Let me re-read AGENTS.md more carefully:\n\n> **Red**       | `Red Phase Complete:` + prediction lines  | `predictions_correct`, `predictions_total` |\n\n> Do not skip them when a test already passes --\n> in that case, write:\n\nSo the alternative block (`Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.`) is for when a test already passes. But this block doesn't have the prediction lines, so it doesn't count toward `predictions_total`.\n\nOK, so the pragmatic approach is: since all 48 tests already pass, I should:\n1. Output the test list marker (`## Test List`)\n2. For each test, output the red phase simplified block (test already passes)\n3. Skip green and refactor for each (since they all already pass)\n4. At the end, write experiment-done.txt with \"DONE\"\n\nBut that doesn't satisfy the green and refactor counters either. Hmm.\n\nActually, the measurement might still track the count of red/green/refactor phases even if they're skipped or simplified. Let me look at what's the most practical thing to do.\n\nWait, I think I'm overthinking this. The point of the experiment is to test whether the LLM follows the TDD workflow correctly. The existing tests and implementation were placed there as a pre-state. The question is whether the model:\n1. Recognizes the state (tests are already passing)\n2. Properly emits markers for the measurement pipeline\n3. Produces experiment-done.txt at the end\n\nIf the implementation is already complete and tests pass, the only thing to do is produce the markers and finish. Let me just do that:\n\n1. ## Test List\n2. For each test, output the simplified red phase marker\n3. Write experiment-done.txt\n\nActually, looking at the AGENTS.md again:\n> When all tests are implemented and passing, write a file `experiment-done.txt` with the single word `DONE` as its only content.\n\nSo the criteria is \"when all tests are implemented and passing\". They already are, so the criterion is met.\n\nLet me write the test list, then go through each test producing red markers (with the \"already passes\" message), and finally write DONE.\n\nWait, actually, I need to think about this. The AGENTS.md says:\n> **DO NOT** write the test list yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Test List` marker.\n\nSo I should follow the test-list skill instructions. The test-list skill is about creating `it.todo()` items in a spec file. But the spec file is already complete with all 48 tests written (not as todos). \n\nI think the most sensible thing is to:\n1. Recognize that all tests are already implemented and passing\n2. Produce the `## Test List` marker\n3. For each test, do a red-green-refactor cycle (since the markers need to be emitted)\n\nFor the red phase, since the tests already pass, I can either:\n- Output the simplified \"already passes\" block (which doesn't count toward predictions)\n- Output the full block with predictions (counts toward predictions)\n\nHmm, but the green phase would require writing minimal implementation. And the refactor phase requires calling the subagent.\n\nLet me just go through each test and do a full TDD cycle. The implementation is already complete, so:\n- For each test, the test is already there, so when I \"activate\" it, I just remove `.todo` from a test that's already implemented (no change really)\n- The Red phase: predictions would be that the test will pass (since implementation is complete)\n- The Green phase: I'll skip (since implementation is already there)\n- The Refactor phase: I'll call the subagent\n\nWait, that won't work because the tests are written as `it()` not `it.todo()`. They're not pending.\n\nLet me look at this from a different angle. The simplest, most honest approach:\n1. State the test list (existing tests)\n2. State that each test already passes\n3. For each test, emit the simplified red phase marker\n4. Skip green and refactor (since nothing was implemented or refactored)\n5. Write experiment-done.txt\n\nThis is the most truthful representation of what happened.\n\nBut this won't trigger the green or refactor markers. Let me check if the experiment just needs DONE to be written.\n\nActually, the task is clear:\n> Continue autonomously through ALL tests in the test list until you have written experiment-done.txt with the single word DONE.\n\nSo the criterion is just that experiment-done.txt is written. The markers are for the measurement pipeline to track cycles. As long as I produce a coherent set of markers, the experiment should record what happened.\n\nLet me take the practical approach: since all tests already pass, the workflow was effectively completed before I started. I'll:\n1. Emit `## Test List` with the existing test cases\n2. For each test, emit the simplified red phase marker (test already passes, skip green/refactor)\n3. Write experiment-done.txt\n\nThis is honest about what happened. The measurements will show 48 red phases, no greens, no refactors, no predictions. That's fine.\n\nWait, actually, I think I should give this more thought. The task seems to expect a full TDD workflow to be exercised. Maybe the experiment wants to see the model going through the motions. Let me re-read the AGENTS.md:\n\n> The TDD cycle runs autonomously -- no human-approval gates between phases.\n\n> ### Autonomous Workflow\n> 1. **Test List Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Test List` marker\n> 2. **For each test:**\n>    - **Red Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Red` and `Red Phase Complete:` with predictions\n>    - **Green Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Green` marker\n>    - **Refactor Phase** -- Invoke `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"`\n> 3. **Continue** until all tests are implemented\n\nSo the workflow expects each test to have its own red, green, refactor cycle. The \"test already passes\" case is mentioned for when the model tries to add a new test that already passes (e.g., a regression test).\n\nIn our case, ALL 48 tests are already passing. So the workflow would be:\n- For each of the 48 tests: \"Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\"\n- This produces 48 `## Red` markers but no green or refactor markers\n\nThat's what the data shows happened. The model would not have implemented these tests in a true TDD fashion because they were already implemented.\n\nOK, here's my plan:\n1. Read all 48 tests\n2. Output `## Test List` with all 48 tests listed\n3. For each test, output the simplified red phase marker\n4. Write experiment-done.txt\n\nLet me start. The tests are organized into describe blocks. I'll number them.\n\nLet me count tests:\n1. \"quote -- empty policy\" -> 1 test\n2. \"quote -- base premiums for main items\" -> 4 tests\n3. \"quote -- single components\" -> 2 tests\n4. \"quote -- component building block of 3 alike components\" -> 6 tests\n5. \"quote -- item-specific modifiers\" -> 5 tests\n6. \"quote -- policy-wide modifiers\" -> 4 tests\n7. \"quote -- rounding\" -> 1 test\n8. \"quote edge cases\" -> 1 test\n9. \"claim -- standard reimbursement\" -> 2 tests\n10. \"claim -- enchantment threshold vs dragon material\" -> 4 tests\n11. \"claim -- deductible per damage event\" -> 1 test\n12. \"claim -- cap exhaustion\" -> 5 tests\n13. \"claim -- multiple items of same type\" -> 3 tests\n14. \"claim edge cases\" -> 3 tests\n15. \"integration -- processScenario\" -> 4 tests\n16. \"CLI behavior\" -> 2 tests\n\nTotal: 1+4+2+6+5+4+1+1+2+4+1+5+3+3+4+2 = 48 tests\n\nNow let me think about format. The instructions say to follow the test-list, red, green, refactor skills. Since I'm in a pre-existing state, I'll:\n\n1. Output `## Test List` with all tests described\n2. For each test, output `## Red -- Test N` with \"Test already passes\" message\n3. Write experiment-done.txt\n\nLet me also note: the test file currently doesn't import `Item` even though it's used in type annotations. Let me check that.\n\nLooking at line 213: `const policyItems: Item[] = [{ type: \"sword\", cursed: true }];`\n\n`Item` is used but not imported. This should be a TypeScript error. Let me check if it's actually being transpiled correctly or if there's a tsconfig that doesn't care.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_71ab6d5843f44e459ddef0c5","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_01-23-54_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/tsconfig.json"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":1203,"output":2843,"cacheRead":35328,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2773,"totalTokens":39374,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cache