 red phase marker\n\nActually, I think I should be more careful. Let me look at this from a totally different angle. \n\nThe task says: \"Continue autonomously through ALL tests in the test list until you have written experiment-done.txt with the single word DONE. Do NOT stop after a single passing test or cycle — keep going until every test is implemented.\"\n\nThe key phrase is \"every test is implemented\". They are all implemented and passing. So I just need to write DONE.\n\nBut the AGENTS.md says:\n> When all tests are implemented and passing, write a file `experiment-done.txt` with the single word `DONE` as its only content. Do not write any other summary or report file.\n\nSo my output should:\n1. Produce the TDD markers for measurement\n2. End with experiment-done.txt containing \"DONE\"\n\nLet me just write the test list marker and go through each test with the simplified red marker. I'll be efficient and not waste time on individual subagent calls since nothing is changing.\n\nActually wait - I should also produce green markers for completeness. Let me look at the marker requirements again:\n- `## Test List` heading - test-list phase occurrence\n- `## Red` heading - red-phase cycle (cycle_count)\n- `## Green` heading - green-phase occurrence\n- `subagent` tool call with agent: \"refactor\" - refactorings_applied\n- `Red Phase Complete:` + prediction lines - predictions_correct, predictions_total\n\nIf I skip green and refactor for all tests (because they all already pass), I'd produce 48 red markers but no green or refactor markers. The cycle_count would be 48, but other metrics would be 0.\n\nAlternatively, I could produce the full cycle for each test:\n- Red marker (with predictions of passing)\n- Green marker\n- Refactor subagent call\n\nThis would produce richer metrics. The challenge is that \"green\" implies I made a change to make the test pass. If nothing needs to change, can I still emit a green marker?\n\nThe red skill says: \"Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\"\n\nSo the prescribed behavior when a test already passes is to skip green/refactor. I should follow this.\n\nOK, here's my final plan:\n1. Output `## Test List` marker\n2. For each of the 48 tests, output `## Red -- Test N` with \"Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\"\n3. Write experiment-done.txt with \"DONE\"\n\nThis follows the skill instructions faithfully and produces the required DONE marker.\n\nLet me also make sure I'm using the exact format. The AGENTS.md says:\n\n> in that case, write:\n> ```\n> ## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n> \n> Test already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\n> ```\n\nOK, I'll use that format. Now let me execute.\n\nWait, the question is - should I count and number them? Yes, I should number them sequentially.\n\nLet me list all 48 tests with descriptions:\n\n1. empty item list -> premium 5 G (only processing fee)\n2. single sword (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 115 G\n3. single amulet (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 71 G\n4. single staff (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 93 G\n5. single potion (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 49 G\n6. single rune (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 33 G\n7. single moonstone (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 33 G\n8. 2 runes -> premium 60 G\n9. 3 runes -> premium 71 G\n10. 4 runes -> premium 115 G\n11. 7 runes -> premium 198 G\n12. 2 runes + 1 moonstone -> premium 88 G\n13. 3 runes + 3 moonstones -> premium 137 G\n14. cursed sword (0 years, 0 contracts) -> premium 165 G\n15. cursed sword + plain amulet -> premium 231 G\n16. sword enchantment 5 -> premium 145 G\n17. sword enchantment 4 -> premium 115 G\n18. cursed sword enchantment 5 -> premium 195 G\n19. customer with exactly 2 years -> premium 95 G\n20. customer with 1 year -> premium 115 G\n21. first insurance always applies -> premium 80 G\n22. follow-up contract -> premium 80 G\n23. premium calculation yielding 197.5 G -> final